# Migrating a Flat Pulumi Program to ComponentResource with Aliases

When refactoring a flat Pulumi TypeScript program into a `ComponentResource`, all resource URNs change because they become nested. Using **`aliases`**, you map old URNs to new ones, ensuring Pulumi adopts existing state instead of replacing resources.

## The Problem

**Before migration (flat):**
```
urn:pulumi:dev:my-project:aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::appBucket
```

**After migration (ComponentResource, without aliases):**
```
urn:pulumi:dev:my-project:custom:infrastructure:Infrastructure::infrastructure$aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::appBucket
```
Pulumi sees this as a new resource and replaces it.

**Solution: Add an alias that maps the new URN to the old one.**

---

## Step-by-Step Migration

### Step 1: Create the ComponentResource Class

```typescript
// infrastructure.ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as eks from "@pulumi/eks";

export interface InfrastructureArgs {
  nodeCount: number;
  nodeInstanceType: string;
  rdsInstanceClass: string;
  rdsMultiAz: boolean;
  s3EnableReplication: boolean;
  region: string;
}

export class Infrastructure extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public eksCluster: eks.Cluster;
  public appBucket: aws.s3.Bucket;
  public rdsInstance: aws.rds.Instance;
  public iamRole: aws.iam.Role;
  public iamUser: aws.iam.User;

  constructor(
    name: string,
    args: InfrastructureArgs,
    opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions
  ) {
    super("custom:infrastructure:Infrastructure", name, {}, opts);

    // EKS Cluster
    this.eksCluster = new eks.Cluster(
      "eksCluster",
      {
        version: "1.27",
        roleName: `${name}-eks-role`,
        nodeGroups: {
          primary: {
            desiredCapacity: args.nodeCount,
            minSize: args.nodeCount,
            maxSize: args.nodeCount * 2,
            instanceType: args.nodeInstanceType,
          },
        },
      },
      { parent: this }
    );

    // S3 Bucket — with alias to prevent replacement
    this.appBucket = new aws.s3.Bucket(
      "appBucket",
      {
        versioning: { enabled: true },
        serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: {
          rule: {
            applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
              sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
            },
          },
        },
        acl: "private",
        forceDestroy: false,
      },
      {
        parent: this,
        // Alias maps this new resource to the old flat program's URN
        aliases: [
          {
            name: "appBucket",
            type: "aws:s3/bucket:Bucket",
          },
        ],
      }
    );

    // S3 Replication (prod only)
    if (args.s3EnableReplication) {
      new aws.s3.BucketReplication(
        "appBucketReplication",
        {
          bucket: this.appBucket.id,
          rules: [
            {
              id: "replicate-all",
              destination: {
                bucket: `arn:aws:s3:::${this.appBucket.id}-replica`,
                storageClass: "STANDARD_IA",
              },
              enabled: true,
              filter: { prefix: "" },
            },
          ],
        },
        { parent: this }
      );
    }

    // RDS PostgreSQL — with alias
    this.rdsInstance = new aws.rds.Instance(
      "postgres",
      {
        engine: "postgres",
        engineVersion: "14.7",
        instanceClass: args.rdsInstanceClass,
        allocatedStorage: args.rdsInstanceClass === "db.t3.micro" ? 20 : 50,
        dbName: "appdb",
        username: "postgres",
        password: pulumi.secret("CHANGE_ME"), // Use ConfigSecret in production
        skipFinalSnapshot: false,
        finalSnapshotIdentifier: `${name}-final-snapshot`,
        multiAz: args.rdsMultiAz,
        deletionProtection: args.rdsMultiAz,
        backupRetentionPeriod: 7,
        publiclyAccessible: false,
        storageEncrypted: true,
        iamDatabaseAuthenticationEnabled: true,
      },
      {
        parent: this,
        aliases: [
          {
            name: "postgres",
            type: "aws:rds/instance:Instance",
          },
        ],
      }
    );

    // IAM Role — with alias
    this.iamRole = new aws.iam.Role(
      "serviceRole",
      {
        assumeRolePolicy: JSON.stringify({
          Version: "2012-10-17",
          Statement: [
            {
              Action: "sts:AssumeRole",
              Effect: "Allow",
              Principal: { Service: "ec2.amazonaws.com" },
            },
          ],
        }),
      },
      {
        parent: this,
        aliases: [
          {
            name: "serviceRole",
            type: "aws:iam/role:Role",
          },
        ],
      }
    );

    // IAM User — with alias
    this.iamUser = new aws.iam.User(
      "appUser",
      {
        name: `${name}-app-user`,
      },
      {
        parent: this,
        aliases: [
          {
            name: "appUser",
            type: "aws:iam/user:User",
          },
        ],
      }
    );

    // Attach least-privilege policy
    new aws.iam.RolePolicyAttachment(
      "serviceRolePolicy",
      {
        role: this.iamRole,
        policyArn: "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/ReadOnlyAccess",
      },
      { parent: this }
    );

    this.registerOutputs({
      eksCluster: this.eksCluster,
      appBucket: this.appBucket,
      rdsInstance: this.rdsInstance,
      iamRole: this.iamRole,
      iamUser: this.iamUser,
    });
  }
}
```

### Step 2: Update Main Program

**Before (flat):**
```typescript
// index.ts
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
const appBucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("appBucket", { versioning: { enabled: true } });
const postgres = new aws.rds.Instance("postgres", { instanceClass: "db.t3.micro" });
const serviceRole = new aws.iam.Role("serviceRole", { /* ... */ });
export const bucketName = appBucket.id;
```

**After (ComponentResource):**
```typescript
// index.ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import { Infrastructure } from "./infrastructure";

const config = new pulumi.Config("infrastructure");
const stack = pulumi.getStack();

const envConfig = {
  dev: {
    nodeCount: 2,
    nodeInstanceType: "t3.medium",
    rdsInstanceClass: "db.t3.micro",
    rdsMultiAz: false,
    s3EnableReplication: false,
    region: "us-east-1",
  },
  prod: {
    nodeCount: 4,
    nodeInstanceType: "t3.large",
    rdsInstanceClass: "db.t3.medium",
    rdsMultiAz: true,
    s3EnableReplication: true,
    region: "us-east-1",
  },
};

const args = envConfig[stack as keyof typeof envConfig] || envConfig.dev;

const infrastructure = new Infrastructure("infrastructure", args);

export const bucketName = infrastructure.appBucket.id;
export const rdsEndpoint = infrastructure.rdsInstance.endpoint;
export const roleArn = infrastructure.iamRole.arn;
export const userId = infrastructure.iamUser.id;
```

### Step 3: Stack Configuration Files

**dev.yaml:**
```yaml
aws:region: us-east-1
```

**prod.yaml:**
```yaml
aws:region: us-east-1
```

(Sizing differences are baked into the component logic via stack name matching, or externalized to config if preferred.)

---

## How Aliases Prevent Replacement

When you run `pulumi up` after the migration:

1. **Pulumi detects**: The new resource `infrastructure$aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::appBucket` has an alias `appBucket` of type `aws:s3/bucket:Bucket`
2. **Pulumi matches**: This alias points to the old state for `appBucket`
3. **Adoption occurs**: Instead of `Delete` + `Create`, Pulumi *adopts* the old resource into the new hierarchy
4. **State update**: The resource remains in place; its URN is updated in the stack

**Preview output (no replacements):**
```
urn:pulumi:dev:my-project:aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::appBucket [adopt]
urn:pulumi:dev:my-project:aws:rds/instance:Instance::postgres [adopt]
urn:pulumi:dev:my-project:aws:iam/role:Role::serviceRole [adopt]
...
```

---

## Deployment Workflow

```bash
# 1. Add aliases to flat program (verify no changes)
pulumi preview   # Should show: no changes

# 2. Commit and deploy with aliases in place
pulumi up        # Adopts state without replacement

# 3. Refactor into ComponentResource
#    (update index.ts to use new Infrastructure class)

# 4. Verify adoption
pulumi preview   # Should show: resource adoptions, not replacements
pulumi up        # Moves resources under component in state

# 5. Verify independently for each stack
pulumi stack select dev
pulumi preview   # Should be empty

pulumi stack select prod
pulumi preview   # Should be empty
```

---

## Verification Checklist

```bash
# Check state file for component hierarchy
pulumi stack export | jq '.deployment.resources | length'

# Verify URNs were updated (old ones should not appear)
pulumi stack export | grep -c 'aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::appBucket' # Should be 0 (now nested)

# Verify outputs still resolve
pulumi stack output bucketName
pulumi stack output rdsEndpoint

# Confirm no resource was deleted/recreated
pulumi stack history | tail -1
```

---

## Key Points

| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| **Alias format** | Use `{ name: "resourceName", type: "aws:service/resource:Class" }` (Pulumi auto-matches across stacks) |
| **Scope** | Add aliases to *every* resource moved into the component |
| **Order** | Deploy with aliases first (no changes), then refactor, then migrate |
| **Dev/Prod safety** | Each stack's adoption is independent; one cannot break the other |
| **Reversibility** | If needed, remove the component and add aliases in reverse |

This approach ensures **zero-downtime refactoring** while maintaining separate dev and prod stacks.
